    body_color: one dominant foreground color (always 1 [observed]), most frequent fg color
    bbox: 3-8 rows x 3-6 cols per sprite
    density: 0.36-0.89 fg cells / bbox area
    structure: irregular shapes with interior bg holes; body + marker pixels all 4-connected
  markers:
    colors: 2-3 distinct marker colors, each != body and != bg
    pixel_count: exactly 1 pixel per marker-color per sprite
    position: always on the bbox boundary, exposed on exactly one edge (top/bottom/left/right)
    adjacency: every marker pixel is 4-adjacent to at least one body pixel
    pairing: each marker color appears in exactly 1 or 2 sprites globally
    paired: appears in 2 sprites with opposite exposed edges (top<->bottom, left<->right)
    unpaired: 0-1 colors appear only once (decorative, not used for docking)
  assembly_tree:
    topology: sprites form a tree via paired markers; #paired_colors == #sprites - 1
    max_degree: up to 3 markers per sprite (hub nodes)
    leaf_degree: 1 paired marker
  input_layout: sprites scattered on bg grid with >=2 cell bbox gap, non-overlapping
  output: tight crop of assembled sprites; paired markers become 4-adjacent; no overlap; all fg preserved

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose N=2-4 sprites. Build each as an irregular 4-connected body (3-8 x 3-6 bbox).
    2. Assign N-1 paired marker colors and 0-1 unpaired marker colors.
    3. Build the assembly tree (random spanning tree over N sprites).
    4. For each tree edge, pick an available boundary cell on each sprite and place matching
       marker pixels on opposite edges (e.g. bottom on sprite A, top on sprite B).
       Place any unpaired marker on a remaining boundary cell of any sprite.
    5. Assemble: position sprites so paired markers are 4-adjacent. Verify no overlap.
       This is the output (tight-cropped).
    6. Scatter the sprites onto a 13x13 bg grid with >=2 cell bbox gap. This is the input.
